1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,500
In 2026, a hospital doctor starts a shift
with a smartphone in one pocket.

2
00:00:05,733 --> 00:00:09,213
And clipped to the belt,
something from the 1980s.

3
00:00:09,413 --> 00:00:13,613
A small plastic box that can only beep
and show a line of text.

4
00:00:14,022 --> 00:00:15,262
A pager.

5
00:00:16,112 --> 00:00:19,432
That little box
has been in hospitals for a long time.

6
00:00:20,172 --> 00:00:26,032
In 1949, an inventor called Al Gross
patented what he called a telephone pager.

7
00:00:26,642 --> 00:00:28,722
The next year, New York's Jewish Hospital

8
00:00:28,952 --> 00:00:31,922
started using his system
to reach its doctors.

9
00:00:32,902 --> 00:00:36,622
That was more than twenty years
before the first mobile phone call.

10
00:00:37,015 --> 00:00:38,675
And it never really left.

11
00:00:39,535 --> 00:00:43,655
In a survey published in 2017
of American hospital clinicians,

12
00:00:44,095 --> 00:00:48,595
almost eight in ten of those who answered
had been given a pager by their hospital.

13
00:00:49,422 --> 00:00:52,362
For about half of all who answered,
it was the main way

14
00:00:52,592 --> 00:00:54,962
messages about patients reached them.

15
00:00:55,491 --> 00:00:59,851
In 2019, the British government
ordered the NHS to stop using pagers

16
00:00:59,951 --> 00:01:03,771
for everyday messages by the end of 2021.

17
00:01:04,411 --> 00:01:07,571
There were about
a hundred and thirty thousand of them.

18
00:01:08,291 --> 00:01:11,491
In the summer of 2026,
they were still in use.

19
00:01:11,763 --> 00:01:13,303
So why won't it go away?

20
00:01:15,023 --> 00:01:18,843
The answer starts with one strange fact
about how a pager works.

21
00:01:19,492 --> 00:01:21,032
It never says anything.

22
00:01:21,652 --> 00:01:24,452
A typical hospital pager
has no transmitter.

23
00:01:24,572 --> 00:01:25,772
It only listens.

24
00:01:27,323 --> 00:01:31,303
What many of them listen for
is a code from the 1970s.

25
00:01:32,163 --> 00:01:36,443
In the mid-1970s, the British Post Office
brought together pager makers

26
00:01:36,673 --> 00:01:41,163
from Britain, Europe and Japan
to agree on one way of paging.

27
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:46,320
The code they worked on is still on the
air today, named after the group: POCSAG.

28
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:48,960
Every pager has its own number.

29
00:01:49,580 --> 00:01:52,400
The network sends that number,
followed by the message.

30
00:01:54,740 --> 00:01:56,480
Your phone works differently.

31
00:01:57,160 --> 00:02:00,200
The network keeps track
of roughly which area it's in.

32
00:02:00,469 --> 00:02:04,749
When a call comes in, the network
calls out across that area to find it.

33
00:02:05,429 --> 00:02:07,669
Engineers even call that paging.

34
00:02:08,011 --> 00:02:09,671
Then your phone answers.

35
00:02:10,331 --> 00:02:13,051
And from that moment on,
it's a two-way conversation.

36
00:02:14,018 --> 00:02:16,978
A one-way pager network
never gets an answer.

37
00:02:17,678 --> 00:02:20,958
It doesn't know where you are,
and it doesn't try to find out.

38
00:02:21,204 --> 00:02:24,744
So it broadcasts every message
across its whole coverage area.

39
00:02:25,404 --> 00:02:29,544
Every pager listens.
Only the one with your number reacts.

40
00:02:31,394 --> 00:02:33,454
That one decision explains
nearly everything

41
00:02:33,684 --> 00:02:38,194
that makes the pager hard to replace.
Start with the battery.

42
00:02:38,852 --> 00:02:40,472
Transmitting is expensive.

43
00:02:41,312 --> 00:02:43,292
This kind of pager never does it.

44
00:02:43,792 --> 00:02:47,192
Its receiver is simple,
and it sleeps most of the time.

45
00:02:47,692 --> 00:02:51,692
It wakes only for its own short slot
in each cycle of the broadcast.

46
00:02:52,371 --> 00:02:55,231
So one small battery
can keep it going for weeks.

47
00:02:55,891 --> 00:02:58,171
A phone needs a charger every day.

48
00:03:00,331 --> 00:03:01,931
Then there's the building.

49
00:03:02,623 --> 00:03:04,963
Hospitals are hard places for radio.

50
00:03:05,163 --> 00:03:07,263
Thick concrete. Basements.

51
00:03:07,493 --> 00:03:12,043
Rooms shielded on purpose,
around scanners and X-ray machines.

52
00:03:12,707 --> 00:03:15,347
A phone needs a link in both directions.

53
00:03:16,087 --> 00:03:19,487
It has to hear the mast,
and the mast has to hear the phone.

54
00:03:20,144 --> 00:03:22,004
The hard half is the phone's.

55
00:03:22,844 --> 00:03:24,404
A small, battery-powered transmitter

56
00:03:24,634 --> 00:03:27,544
has to reach a mast
that may be kilometres away.

57
00:03:28,263 --> 00:03:30,483
A pager only needs the other half.

58
00:03:31,183 --> 00:03:34,043
That transmitter can be powerful,
and mounted high up.

59
00:03:34,803 --> 00:03:38,303
And it sends slowly,
a few thousand bits a second at most.

60
00:03:39,009 --> 00:03:41,889
A slower signal can hold up better
in noisy conditions.

61
00:03:42,119 --> 00:03:45,069
Together with power and frequency,

62
00:03:45,149 --> 00:03:49,169
that often lets a paging network reach
places where phones struggle.

63
00:03:51,559 --> 00:03:54,719
The third advantage
only shows up on the worst day.

64
00:03:55,524 --> 00:03:59,384
When disaster strikes,
everyone reaches for a phone at once.

65
00:04:00,144 --> 00:04:04,124
And every call puts another demand
on a network that's already overloaded.

66
00:04:04,838 --> 00:04:09,638
After Hurricane Katrina in 2005,
Verizon said that where phones

67
00:04:09,868 --> 00:04:13,058
still worked at all,
the circuits were often jammed.

68
00:04:13,830 --> 00:04:17,390
Pager networks have limits too,
and their masts can fail.

69
00:04:18,150 --> 00:04:20,250
But a thousand people receiving
the same message

70
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:22,230
doesn't mean a thousand connections.

71
00:04:23,150 --> 00:04:24,950
It's still just one broadcast.

72
00:04:27,450 --> 00:04:31,370
That's the fourth advantage,
the one hospitals care about most.

73
00:04:32,047 --> 00:04:35,367
When a patient's heart stops,
a whole team has to come running.

74
00:04:35,827 --> 00:04:38,747
Doctors, an anaesthetist, several nurses.

75
00:04:39,505 --> 00:04:43,225
In many British hospitals,
someone dials 2222.

76
00:04:43,785 --> 00:04:47,365
And every pager on that team
goes off in the same second.

77
00:04:48,009 --> 00:04:51,009
No code to enter. No app to open.

78
00:04:51,329 --> 00:04:53,309
No group to put together first.

79
00:04:53,995 --> 00:04:56,715
For decades, that was the whole case
for the pager.

80
00:04:57,455 --> 00:04:59,215
It can reach people across the building,

81
00:04:59,295 --> 00:05:01,695
all at once,
without needing them to answer.

82
00:05:04,615 --> 00:05:06,795
But that same decision has a price.

83
00:05:07,570 --> 00:05:11,530
Because the pager can't talk,
it can't confirm the message arrived.

84
00:05:11,990 --> 00:05:15,750
The nurse who sends a page
doesn't know if the doctor saw it.

85
00:05:15,987 --> 00:05:17,747
And the doctor can't reply.

86
00:05:18,227 --> 00:05:20,227
They have to find a phone and call back...

87
00:05:20,307 --> 00:05:22,807
by which time the nurse may have moved on.

88
00:05:23,359 --> 00:05:26,619
That's the first price.
The second is worse.

89
00:05:27,740 --> 00:05:30,520
A message sent everywhere
can be heard everywhere.

90
00:05:31,271 --> 00:05:35,011
Some pager systems
still send their messages as plain text.

91
00:05:35,571 --> 00:05:37,391
Encrypted paging exists.

92
00:05:38,051 --> 00:05:40,031
But these systems don't use it.

93
00:05:40,544 --> 00:05:45,184
With a cheap radio receiver, anyone
in range can pick those messages up.

94
00:05:45,739 --> 00:05:50,159
In 2018, researchers in Vancouver
found hospital pages on the air

95
00:05:50,389 --> 00:05:54,039
with patients' names,
diagnoses and room numbers.

96
00:05:54,731 --> 00:05:58,931
In August 2026,
the BBC found that the NHS service

97
00:05:59,161 --> 00:06:03,031
that matches donated organs
had been sending transplant patients'

98
00:06:03,261 --> 00:06:07,191
names, birth dates and organ details
the same way.

99
00:06:07,712 --> 00:06:08,212
It apologised.

100
00:06:08,292 --> 00:06:12,412
And stopped sending
patients' personal details over the air.

101
00:06:14,862 --> 00:06:16,782
You'd think that settles it.

102
00:06:17,002 --> 00:06:19,042
Hospitals now have secure messaging apps.

103
00:06:19,272 --> 00:06:23,602
Two-way, encrypted,
with a tick when your message is seen.

104
00:06:23,921 --> 00:06:26,321
And for most messages,
the apps are winning.

105
00:06:27,101 --> 00:06:29,381
Could you check the blood results
in bed twelve?

106
00:06:30,081 --> 00:06:33,801
Can this patient move to the ward?
Those are conversations.

107
00:06:34,821 --> 00:06:36,601
A phone is better at conversations.

108
00:06:37,699 --> 00:06:42,859
In 2022, Cambridge University Hospitals
made exactly that switch.

109
00:06:43,598 --> 00:06:46,498
It upgraded its Wi-Fi
to cover the whole site.

110
00:06:47,238 --> 00:06:51,028
For the first week,
messages went to the app and the pagers.

111
00:06:51,398 --> 00:06:54,718
After that, everyday pages
dropped almost to nothing.

112
00:06:55,103 --> 00:06:57,863
But three kinds of call
were never meant to move.

113
00:06:58,203 --> 00:07:01,923
Cardiac arrest. Major trauma. Fire.

114
00:07:02,564 --> 00:07:05,424
Even the old pager system
wasn't switched off.

115
00:07:06,124 --> 00:07:10,284
It would stay in place until a resilience
review showed it was safe to remove.

116
00:07:12,404 --> 00:07:15,424
These are the calls
where nobody has time to reply.

117
00:07:15,624 --> 00:07:19,984
Where the message has to reach
a whole team at once, wherever they are.

118
00:07:23,094 --> 00:07:24,814
And that's the real answer.

119
00:07:25,394 --> 00:07:27,894
A hospital isn't sending
one kind of message.

120
00:07:27,974 --> 00:07:29,214
It's sending two.

121
00:07:29,827 --> 00:07:31,327
Most of them are questions.

122
00:07:31,967 --> 00:07:35,027
A question has to arrive,
be seen, and get an answer.

123
00:07:35,667 --> 00:07:37,307
A phone does all three.

124
00:07:37,948 --> 00:07:39,428
A few of them are alarms.

125
00:07:40,108 --> 00:07:42,888
An alarm has to reach a whole group,
right now.

126
00:07:43,328 --> 00:07:46,308
Nobody needs to reply. They need to run.

127
00:07:46,903 --> 00:07:48,463
Those are different jobs.

128
00:07:48,643 --> 00:07:51,243
So it makes sense to use different tools.

129
00:07:52,593 --> 00:07:54,953
It isn't that the pager is unbreakable.

130
00:07:55,653 --> 00:07:57,853
A modern hospital can have backup power,

131
00:07:57,933 --> 00:08:01,173
spare Wi-Fi,
and emergency systems of its own.

132
00:08:01,736 --> 00:08:03,956
What the pager offers is another channel.

133
00:08:04,036 --> 00:08:07,996
Simpler, different,
and one that can fail in different ways.

134
00:08:08,725 --> 00:08:11,665
For an alarm,
that matters more than cleverness.

135
00:08:12,405 --> 00:08:16,245
You want the channel that doesn't fail
at the same moment as everything else.

136
00:08:17,287 --> 00:08:18,667
For more than seventy years,

137
00:08:18,747 --> 00:08:21,887
that channel has been a little box
that can't say a word.

138
00:08:24,697 --> 00:08:26,757
Which leaves an awkward question.

139
00:08:27,681 --> 00:08:32,041
Outside hospitals,
almost everyone has left the pager behind.

140
00:08:32,381 --> 00:08:36,921
Japan switched off
its last commercial pager service in 2019.

141
00:08:37,656 --> 00:08:42,316
Fewer customers mean fewer networks,
fewer companies making the devices,

142
00:08:42,636 --> 00:08:44,856
fewer people who know how to fix them.

143
00:08:45,025 --> 00:08:47,185
If that goes on, hospitals may become

144
00:08:47,415 --> 00:08:50,085
some of the last customers
of a whole technology.

145
00:08:51,225 --> 00:08:54,245
And a network with few customers
is hard to keep running.

146
00:08:54,673 --> 00:08:56,273
So whatever replaces the pager

147
00:08:56,503 --> 00:09:00,073
for those last few calls
can't just be a better phone.

148
00:09:00,457 --> 00:09:02,817
It has to keep the pager's oldest trick.

149
00:09:02,917 --> 00:09:04,917
Reach everyone at once.

150
00:09:05,017 --> 00:09:07,397
Keep working when other things stop.

151
00:09:07,597 --> 00:09:09,217
And say as little as possible.

152
00:09:11,127 --> 00:09:13,007
So here's a question for you.

153
00:09:13,587 --> 00:09:16,727
In an emergency, would you rather
get a message you can answer...

154
00:09:17,047 --> 00:09:20,027
or one designed to reach you
when everything else fails?

155
00:09:20,667 --> 00:09:21,877
Let me know in the comments.
